UMAP-style objectives interpret a weighted graph as the 1-skeleton of a fuzzy simplicial set (FSS): an edge set with graded memberships μij∈[0,1] egin{document}$\mu_{ij}\in[0,1]$\end{document} that encode how strongly i egin{document}$i$\end{document} and j egin{document}$j$\end{document} belong to one another’s neighborhood. Given a low-dimensional embedding Y={yi} egin{document}$Y=\{y_{i}\}$\end{document}, a second fuzzy edge set with memberships νij(Y)∈[0,1] egin{document}$
u_{ij}(Y)\in[0,1]$\end{document} is induced by a fixed, monotonically decreasing function of the low-dimensional distance ‖yi−yj‖ egin{document}$\|y_{i}-y_{j}\|$\end{document}. The layout is obtained by minimizing the cross-entropy between the two fuzzy edge sets:ℒUMAP⁢(Y)=∑i<j[μi⁢j⁢log⁡μi⁢jνi⁢j⁢(Y)+(1-μi⁢j)⁢log⁡1-μi⁢j1-νi⁢j⁢(Y)]. egin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{UMAP}}(Y)\;=\;\sum_{i \lt j}\Big{[}\mu_{ij}\,\log rac{\mu_{ ij}}{
u_{ij}(Y)}\;+\; ig{(}1-\mu_{ij} ig{)}\,\log rac{1-\mu_{ij}}{1-
u_{ ij}(Y)}\Big{]}.$$\end{document}
